  "reasoning": "The patient presents with fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, weakness, and fatigue. These symptoms collectively indicate an acute viral infection triggering a systemic immune response. Swollen lymph nodes (particularly cervical), along with fever and headache, are hallmark signs of a viral upper respiratory infection. Evaluating the provided options: chronic conditions (diabetes, hypertension, psoriasis, etc.) do not match; vector-borne illnesses like dengue or malaria typically present with severe joint/body pain, rash, or cyclical chills, which are absent; typhoid usually features prolonged fever and gastrointestinal distress. The triad of fever, headache, and swollen lymph nodes, accompanied by general fatigue and concentration difficulties due to fever, aligns most closely with a viral upper respiratory infection. Reference patterns similarly associate swollen lymph nodes and fatigue with the common cold. Therefore, the clinical picture best fits the common cold.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]common cold[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
